 look at this as a comparison between  other english-speaking countries and  western european countries and we  compare them to canada canada ranks dead  last there isn't actually that many  other areas that you can find canada  ranks dead last right so it is very  clear and and actually just to emphasize  this point this includes Quebec  right so if you move outside of the  Quebec area that where you have a  program in place the canadian numbers  even look worse so what would it take to  get canada to the average of OECD  nations not above it that's just the  average well the answer is it would cost  about three to four billion dollars and  then the question is okay so now we have  governments in Canada that are working  against binding fiscal constraints right  the the federal government probably will  balance its budget in 2015 if not 2014 I  personally think it's gonna be 2014 but  the provinces you know many of the  provinces like Ontario actually faced  much tougher fiscal challenges and from  an advocacy point of view I think that  the message here is you we need to drive  home the point the value of of  investment in the space but when you're  talking to the government you also have  to be pragmatic in terms of  understanding what the fiscal  constraints are going to be but in terms  of making the case what you really need  to do is make the case that there's a  more efficient way of using the  resources we have and from a point of  view of you know when as balanced as  budgets are being balanced and  governments are looking at reforming  their spending you know they should be  thinking about where are they going to  get the biggest bang for their buck and  in my opinion early childhood education  is one of those areas where it will have  it would have a very large long-term  impact I often get pushed back about you  know should you spend three or four  billion dollars on early childhood  education or should you spend it on  skills development for for workers today  to deal with shortages today and the  answer is yes right you have to do both  right businesses are going to need  workers today but we're also going to  need the workers of the future and you  need to be able to balance that if  you're going to be successful in the  long run  take left we were going to overcome a  lot of the other challenges that we have  to face so I'll stop there and I look  forward to any questions  you
 

